---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const updated = '10 September 2026';
---
<Layout
	title="Privacy Policy — ConfigButler"
	description="ConfigButler's privacy policy. We collect the minimum we need, we never sell or rent personal information, and you can have your data deleted by sending one email."
>
	<!-- HERO -->
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="hero-bg"></div>
		<div class="wrap" style="max-width:900px">
			<span class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span>privacy</span>
			<h1 class="display" style="max-width:14ch;margin-top:18px">
				Privacy <span class="b">policy.</span>
			</h1>
			<p class="lede" style="margin-top:22px;max-width:640px">
				We collect the least we can get away with, we use it only to do the thing you asked for, and
				we do not sell, rent, or trade personal information to anyone. Ever.
			</p>
			<p class="mono" style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-3);margin-top:18px">
				last updated {updated}
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="wrap"><div class="hr"></div></div>

	<!-- THE SHORT VERSION -->
	<section class="sect sect--paper-2 tight">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="grid grid-4">
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="lock" /></span><div class="ft">Never sold</div><div class="fd">Your personal information is not sold, rented, traded, or shared for advertising.</div></div>
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="scan" /></span><div class="ft">No tracking</div><div class="fd">No analytics, no advertising cookies, no profiling, no third-party trackers on this site.</div></div>
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="doc" /></span><div class="ft">Minimum data</div><div class="fd">Your name, your email, and whatever you chose to tell us. That's the whole list.</div></div>
				<div class="f rv"><span class="fi"><Icon name="x" /></span><div class="ft">Deleted on request</div><div class="fd">Email us and your personal data goes away — bar the change history you asked us to keep.</div></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- THE POLICY -->
	<section class="sect">
		<div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose rv">
			<h2>Who we are</h2>
			<p>
				ConfigButler is a founder-led company run by Simon Koudijs, based in the Netherlands.
				For anything in this policy, write to
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a> — it reaches a
				person, not a ticket queue.
			</p>

			<h2>What we collect</h2>
			<p>There are exactly four ways we end up with your personal information.</p>
			<h3>If you contact us</h3>
			<p>
				The contact form takes your <strong>name</strong>, <strong>email address</strong>, and the
				<strong>message</strong> you write. Submissions are delivered to our inbox by
				<a class="link" href="https://web3forms.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Web3Forms</a>,
				which passes the message on and does not use it for anything else.
			</p>
			<h3>If you sign in with LinkedIn</h3>
			<p>
				When you use <strong>Sign in with LinkedIn</strong>, LinkedIn sends us only the basic profile
				fields you approve on LinkedIn's own consent screen: your name, profile headline, profile
				picture, and email address. We use them to create your account and to know who is signed in.
			</p>
			<p>
				We do not post on your behalf, we do not read your feed, your messages, or your connections,
				and we do not request access to them. You can revoke our access at any time from
				<strong>Settings &amp; Privacy → Data privacy → Permitted services</strong> on LinkedIn.
			</p>
			<h3>If you use ConfigButler to change configuration</h3>
			<p>
				This one is the product, not a side effect. ConfigButler exists to keep an honest record of
				who changed what, so <strong>every change you make is stored together with your name and
				your email address</strong>, the time you made it, and exactly what you changed.
			</p>
			<p>
				That record is written to your organisation's own Git repository as an ordinary commit, with
				you as the commit author — the same attribution any Git history carries. It is what makes the
				history reviewable and auditable, and it is the reason people can trust the record later.
			</p>
			<p>
				We use that attribution for nothing else. It is not analysed, not scored, and never shared
				outside your organisation.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>That Git repository is yours, and keeping it safe is your responsibility.</strong> It
				lives where you chose to put it — GitHub, GitLab, your own server — and you decide who can
				clone, read, or fork it. Anyone you grant read access can see every name and email address in
				the history, and if you make the repository public, that attribution is public too. Access
				control, credentials, and backups for that repository are yours to manage, not ours.
			</p>

			<h3>If you just visit the site</h3>
			<p>
				This website is static and hosted on GitHub Pages, which keeps short-lived server logs
				(including IP addresses) for security and abuse prevention. Fonts are loaded from Google
				Fonts, so your browser's request reaches Google. We set no cookies of our own and run no
				analytics here.
			</p>

			<h2>What we use it for</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>To reply to you when you get in touch.</li>
				<li>To authenticate you and run your account, if you have one.</li>
				<li>To attribute each configuration change to the person who made it.</li>
				<li>To keep the service working and secure.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				That is the complete list. We do not use your information to build advertising profiles, and
				we will not use it for a new purpose without asking you first.
			</p>

			<h2>What we never do</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>We do not sell, rent, or trade your personal information — to anyone, for any
				price.</strong> We do not share it with advertisers, ad networks, or data brokers. We do not
				sell it as part of a "partnership", a "data collaboration", or any other rewording of the same
				thing. There is no version of this business where your personal data is the product.
			</p>

			<h2>Who we share it with</h2>
			<p>
				Only the small set of providers we need to operate, and only so they can do their job for us:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>GitHub Pages</strong> — hosts this website.</li>
				<li><strong>Web3Forms</strong> — delivers contact form submissions to our inbox.</li>
				<li><strong>LinkedIn</strong> — handles sign-in, if you choose to use it.</li>
				<li><strong>Our email provider</strong> — carries the mail we exchange with you.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Beyond that, we disclose personal information only if the law requires it. If ConfigButler is
				ever acquired, we will say so here before your data moves anywhere.
			</p>

			<h2>How long we keep it</h2>
			<p>
				Contact emails are kept while the conversation is useful, and deleted when it isn't. Account
				data is kept while your account exists and is removed when you delete it or ask us to.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Change history is the deliberate exception.</strong> An audit trail that could be
				quietly rewritten would be worthless, so the name and email recorded against a change stay in
				your organisation's Git history even after you close your account. That history lives in your
				organisation's repository and is theirs to keep or erase — if you need your attribution
				removed from it, ask them, and we will help them do it.
			</p>

			<h2>Your rights</h2>
			<p>
				Under the GDPR you can ask us to give you a copy of your data, correct it, export it, delete
				it, or stop using it. Send one email to
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a> and we will sort
				it out within 30 days, usually a lot sooner. You also have the right to complain to your local
				data protection authority — in the Netherlands, the Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens.
			</p>

			<h2>Cookies</h2>
			<p>
				This website uses no cookies at all. If you sign in to a ConfigButler application, we use a
				strictly necessary session cookie to keep you signed in. Nothing on this site is used for
				tracking or advertising.
			</p>

			<h2>Security</h2>
			<p>
				Everything is served over HTTPS, access to personal data is limited to people who need it, and
				we hold as little of it as possible — the most reliable way to protect data is not to have
				collected it.
			</p>
			<p>
				That covers the parts we run. Your own Git repository sits outside it: we can write an honest
				record into it, but we cannot protect a repository we do not control. Securing it is on you.
			</p>

			<h2>Children</h2>
			<p>
				ConfigButler is a tool for software teams and is not directed at anyone under 16. We do not
				knowingly collect their personal information.
			</p>

			<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
			<p>
				If this policy changes, we update the date at the top of this page. Material changes that
				affect how we handle your data will be flagged to account holders by email.
			</p>

			<h2>Contact</h2>
			<p>
				Questions, requests, or concerns:
				<a class="link" href="mailto:simon@configbutler.ai">simon@configbutler.ai</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
